---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
title="Why Systems?"
description="A perspective for understanding the world."
>

    <meta
      name="description"
      content="A perspective for understanding the world through systems thinking."
    />

    <style>
      * {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        box-sizing: border-box;
      }

      html {
        font-size: 16px;
      }

      body {
        font-family: Inter, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI",
          sans-serif;
        background: #ffffff;
        color: #111111;
        padding: 80px 24px;
      }

      article {
        max-width: 760px;
        margin: 0 auto;
      }

      .back {
        margin-bottom: 56px;
        font-size: 1rem;
      }

      a {
        color: inherit;
        text-decoration: none;
        border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
      }

      a:hover {
        border-color: #111;
      }

      h1 {
        font-size: 3.5rem;
        line-height: 1.05;
        letter-spacing: -0.06em;
        margin-bottom: 16px;
      }

      .subtitle {
        color: #666;
        font-size: 1.15rem;
        margin-bottom: 72px;
      }

      h2 {
        font-size: 2rem;
        margin-top: 72px;
        margin-bottom: 24px;
        letter-spacing: -0.03em;
      }

      p {
        font-size: 1.15rem;
        line-height: 1.9;
        color: #444;
        margin-bottom: 28px;
      }

      blockquote {
        margin: 48px 0;
        padding-left: 24px;
        border-left: 3px solid #111;
        font-size: 1.5rem;
        line-height: 1.5;
        color: #111;
      }

      ul {
        margin: 24px 0 40px 28px;
      }

      li {
        margin-bottom: 16px;
        font-size: 1.1rem;
        line-height: 1.8;
        color: #444;
      }

      hr {
        border: none;
        border-top: 1px solid #eee;
        margin: 72px 0;
      }

      .closing {
        font-size: 1.35rem;
        color: #111;
        margin-top: 48px;
        line-height: 1.6;
      }

      @media (max-width: 768px) {
        h1 {
          font-size: 2.6rem;
        }

        h2 {
          font-size: 1.6rem;
        }

        p,
        li {
          font-size: 1rem;
        }

        blockquote {
          font-size: 1.25rem;
        }
      }
    </style>
  </head>

  <body>

    <article>

      <div class="back">
        <a href="/">← Back to 84Hz</a>
      </div>

      <h1>Why Systems?</h1>

      <p class="subtitle">
        A perspective for understanding the world.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most of us experience the world one event at a time.
      </p>

      <p>
        A product launch fails. An AI model produces an unexpected response. A
        security breach makes the headlines. A talented employee resigns. A
        company that once moved quickly suddenly becomes slow.
      </p>

      <p>
        These events capture our attention because they are visible. They are
        immediate. They demand an explanation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Our instinct is to ask:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        What happened?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        It is a natural question.
      </p>

      <p>
        But it is rarely the most useful one.
      </p>

      <hr>

      <h2>Events Are Visible. Systems Are Not.</h2>

      <p>
        Behind every visible event lies an invisible system.
      </p>

      <p>
        Products are shaped by architectures. Organizations by incentives.
        Markets by rules. Careers by habits. AI by data, objectives,
        governance, evaluation, and human oversight.
      </p>

      <p>
        By the time we notice an outcome, the system has often been producing
        it for months—or years.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        Events are where systems become visible.
      </blockquote>

      <hr>

      <h2>The Limits of Event Thinking</h2>

      <p>
        Imagine a software team that repeatedly misses deadlines.
      </p>

      <p>
        We could conclude that the team simply needs to work harder.
      </p>

      <p>
        A systems perspective asks different questions.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>What incentives shape behaviour?</li>
        <li>Where are the bottlenecks?</li>
        <li>Which feedback loops reinforce the problem?</li>
        <li>What constraints exist that nobody is talking about?</li>
        <li>How could the system itself be redesigned?</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The objective shifts from fixing today's problem to designing a better
        tomorrow.
      </p>

      <hr>

      <h2>Every System Produces Its Outcomes</h2>

      <blockquote>
        Every system is perfectly designed to get the results it gets.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Whether discussing organizations, products, AI, governments, or
        careers, repeated outcomes are rarely accidental.
      </p>

      <p>
        Incentives create behaviour.
      </p>

      <p>
        Feedback loops reinforce patterns.
      </p>

      <p>
        Constraints shape decisions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Systems quietly determine what becomes easy, difficult, rewarded, or
        ignored.
      </p>

      <hr>

      <h2>Seeing Systems Everywhere</h2>

      <p>
        Once you begin thinking this way, you cannot unsee it.
      </p>

      <p>
        AI is not simply about models. It is about governance, evaluation,
        trust, incentives, observability, and people.
      </p>

      <p>
        Engineering is not simply about code. It is about architecture,
        interfaces, ownership, and evolution.
      </p>

      <p>
        Leadership is not simply about people. It is about designing
        environments where good decisions become the natural outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        Careers are not built from isolated opportunities. They emerge from
        years of curiosity, deliberate practice, relationships, and learning.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        Different domains.<br />
        The same underlying principles.
      </blockquote>

      <hr>

      <h2>The Questions Worth Asking</h2>

      <p>
        Systems thinking changes the questions we ask.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>What conditions made this outcome likely?</li>
        <li>What incentives are driving this behaviour?</li>
        <li>Which feedback loops are reinforcing it?</li>
        <li>Where is the real leverage point?</li>
        <li>How do we redesign the system instead of reacting to its symptoms?</li>
      </ul>

      <hr>

      <h2>Why 84Hz Exists</h2>

      <p>
        84Hz exists to explore the systems that shape our world.
      </p>

      <p>
        Through essays, research, frameworks, and experiments, this studio
        examines artificial intelligence, engineering, governance,
        organizations, technology, and careers through a single lens:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        Better systems create better outcomes.
      </blockquote>

      <p class="closing">
        The topics will evolve.<br />
        The perspective will remain the same.
      </p>

    </article>

  </body>
</html>